WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.465
Autoescaping is a framework feature that helps encode template variables
so they are less likely to be interpreted as markup in common output
contexts.

2
00:00:08.465 --> 00:00:11.971
It is a strong default, especially for ordinary HTML body text.

3
00:00:11.971 --> 00:00:14.550
But secure output handling is context-aware.

4
00:00:14.550 --> 00:00:24.536
HTML body text, HTML attributes, URLs, JavaScript strings, JSON, and CSS
each have different browser parsing rules and may require different
constraints or rendering patterns.

5
00:00:24.536 --> 00:00:27.579
Escaping and sanitization are related but different.

6
00:00:27.579 --> 00:00:34.126
Escaping encodes data for a particular output context so the browser
treats it as data rather than markup or behavior.

7
00:00:34.126 --> 00:00:42.129
Sanitization applies a content policy, often to rich content, by
preserving allowed formatting while removing or neutralizing unsafe parts.

8
00:00:42.129 --> 00:00:50.462
Input validation is also different: it limits what the application
accepts, but it does not automatically make every future rendering context
safe.

9
00:00:50.462 --> 00:00:54.165
Disabling or bypassing escaping should require explicit review.

10
00:00:54.165 --> 00:01:04.218
Marking content as safe, using raw filters, returning prebuilt markup from
helpers, or building template fragments dynamically can all change the
security meaning of a template.

11
00:01:04.218 --> 00:01:10.765
Sometimes an application truly needs trusted markup, but that should come
from a reviewed source with a clear policy.

12
00:01:10.765 --> 00:01:14.337
Convenience is not a good reason to weaken a rendering boundary.

13
00:01:14.337 --> 00:01:17.908
The practical rule is to handle output at the point of rendering.

14
00:01:17.908 --> 00:01:30.540
Ask what context the value enters, whether the value is trusted for that
context, whether framework autoescaping still applies, whether a vetted
sanitizer is needed, and whether the response exposes more data than
necessary.

15
00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:36.360
This discipline keeps secure rendering durable even as templates, APIs,
and frontend behavior evolve.
